      "en": "## Context & Discovery\nAt the summit of the Sulztalferner glacier, 2,955â€¯m above sea level in Austriaâ€™s Tyrol region, a hiker made an unexpected find in July: a backpack, an ice axe, a camera and a pipe dating from the 1950sâ€‘60s. The gear belonged to an 86â€‘yearâ€‘old mountaineer who, in 1963, was forced to leave it behind after an avalanche trapped him.\n\n## The survivorâ€™s testimony\n> \"I never imagined I would see my pack again after all these years,\" the mountaineer said, visibly moved when presented with the recovered items.\n\n## Climate changeâ€™s role\nSulztalferner, like many Alpine glaciers, is receding rapidly due to global warming. Austrian Meteorological Institute data show an average loss of 1.2â€¯% of glacial surface each year, exposing artifacts long buried beneath ice.\n\n## Historical significance\nThese relics provide a rare glimpse into postâ€‘war mountaineering equipment and remind us of the extreme conditions early alpinists faced. They also fuel public debate on the speed of climate change and its impact on natural and cultural heritage.\n\n## Looking ahead\nAs glaciers continue to melt, more historical objects may surface, offering historians and climate scientists fresh data sources. The 60â€‘yearâ€‘old backpack case underscores both climate vulnerability and the enduring power of personal memory."
