 de ces acteurs sur la politique Ã©nergÃ©tique rÃ©gionale pour les dÃ©cennies Ã  venir.",
      "en": "In the diplomatic corridors of Nairobi and Kampala, a quiet yet intense competition is unfolding for control of the petroleum value chain. As East Africa seeks to reduce its reliance on imported refined products, major players such as the Dangote Group, trader Vitol, and the Emirati firm Alpha MBM are positioning themselves to capture opportunities tied to new regional refinery projects.\n\n## A Strategic Reconfiguration\nThe stakes extend beyond mere industrial profitability. For the governments of the East African Community (EAC), the goal is to secure energy supplies against the backdrop of global market volatility. Constructing local refineries is viewed as an essential lever to stabilize trade balances and support the region's nascent industrialization.\n\n> \"Mastering our refining chain is the foundation of our future economic independence. We can no longer afford to rely exclusively on external markets for our fuel needs,\" notes an energy expert based in Dar es-Salaam.\n\n## The Contenders\nThe Dangote Group, bolstered by its Nigerian experience, brings expertise in vertical integration. Conversely, Vitol leverages its logistical prowess and global distribution network, while Alpha MBM offers innovative financing models for heavy infrastructure. Each candidate is attempting to convince local authorities of the viability of their technical and financial models.\n\n## Regional Outlook\nThe final decision by host states will not be purely technical. It will reflect a complex arbitration between national security requirements, financing capabilities, and geopolitical alliances. The choice of partner will determine not only the cost at the pump for the population but also the influence these actors will wield over regional energy policy for decades to come."
