©itÃ©rant ces principes, le Khalife gÃ©nÃ©ral des Mourides renforce le contrat social tacite qui lie les institutions religieuses Ã  l'Ã‰tat, assurant que la spiritualitÃ© reste un levier de cohÃ©sion nationale plutÃ´t qu'un facteur de division.",
      "en": "In the holy city of Touba, the spiritual epicenter of Mouridism, the 2026 Maouloud celebration took on a solemn dimension. Serigne Ahmadou Badawi MbackÃ©, acting as the designated spokesperson for the occasion, unveiled the message from the Caliph General, Serigne Mountakha MbackÃ©, emphasizing the necessity of preserving social stability and harmony among Senegalese citizens. This address comes at a time when religious authorities serve as essential social regulators in the face of contemporary challenges.\n\n## A Call for Civic Responsibility\nThe Caliph's message extended beyond the spiritual significance of commemorating the Prophet's birth. He underscored the importance of ethics in both public and private life, urging the faithful to cultivate tolerance and dialogue. The Caliph reminded the nation that peace is the foundation upon which all development rests, inviting political actors and civil society to prioritize the nation's higher interests.\n\n> \"Preserving social peace is a sacred duty incumbent upon every citizen, regardless of political or social affiliations,\" stated Serigne Ahmadou Badawi MbackÃ© on behalf of the Caliph.\n\n## Institutional and Religious Stakes\nTouba's influence extends far beyond the borders of the Mouride community. As a moral authority, the Caliph General exerts a stabilizing influence on the Senegalese political landscape. This message, delivered ahead of significant upcoming events, is interpreted by observers as a benevolent call to order, aimed at easing potential tensions and encouraging governance focused on the welfare of the population.\n\n## Perspectives for National Stability\nThe significance of this address aligns with a long-standing tradition of mediation where Senegalese religious leaders act as guarantors of stability. By reiterating these principles, the Mouride Caliph General reinforces the tacit social contract linking religious institutions to the State, ensuring that spirituality remains a lever for national cohesion rather than a source of division."
